                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  2850'                         EXADDR:
  2850'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2851'   32 2E9B'              	STA	SRNGF
  2854'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2855'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  2857'   CA 2880'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  285A'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  285B'   CD 2895'              	CALL	EXNUM
  285E'   DA 287E'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2861'   22 2E9C'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2864'   22 2E9E'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  2867'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2868'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  286A'   C2 2877'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  286D'   13                    	INX	D
  286E'   CD 2895'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2871'   DA 287E'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  2874'   22 2E9E'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2877'                         EXA_YS:
  2877'   EB                    	XCHG
  2878'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  287A'   32 2E9B'              	STA	SRNGF
  287D'   C9                    	RET
  287E'                         EXA_NO:
  287E'   EB                    	XCHG
  287F'   C9                    	RET
  2880'                         EXA_AL:
  2880'   23                    	INX	H
  2881'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2882'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2885'   22 2E9C'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2888'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  288B'   22 2E9E'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  288E'   E1                    	POP	H
  288F'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2891'   32 2E9B'              	STA	SRNGF
  2894'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


                                ;digit there at all.
  2895'                         EXNUM:
  2895'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2896'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2898'   D8                    	RC
  2899'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  289B'   3F                    	CMC
  289C'   D8                    	RC
  289D'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  28A0'                         EXN_LP:
  28A0'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  28A1'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  28A3'   DA 28BD'              	JC	EXN_DN
  28A6'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  28A8'   D2 28BD'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  28AB'   13                    	INX	D
  28AC'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  28AD'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  28AE'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  28B0'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  28B1'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  28B2'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  28B3'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  28B4'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  28B5'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  28B6'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  28B7'   D1                    	POP	D
  28B8'   09                    	DAD	B
  28B9'   C1                    	POP	B
  28BA'   C3 28A0'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  28BD'                         EXN_DN:
  28BD'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  28BE'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  28BF'                         EX_S:
  28BF'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  28C0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28C1'   CA 2903'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  28C4'   32 2EA0'              	STA	SDELIM
  28C7'   23                    	INX	H
  28C8'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  28CB'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  28CE'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  28CF'   CD 2909'              	CALL	SCOPY
  28D2'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  28D3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28D4'   CA 2903'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  28D7'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  28DA'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  28DC'   32 2E68'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  28DF'   11 2EB2'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  28E2'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  28E4'   CD 2909'              	CALL	SCOPY
  28E7'   78                    	MOV	A,B
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-127


  28E8'   32 2EB1'              	STA	SREPLN
  28EB'   AF                    	XRA	A
  28EC'   32 2EA1'              	STA	SGFLAG
  28EF'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  28F0'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  28F2'   C2 28FB'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  28F5'   23                    	INX	H
  28F6'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  28F8'   32 2EA1'              	STA	SGFLAG
  28FB'                         EX_SG:
  28FB'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  28FC'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28FD'   C2 268A'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  2900'   C3 2929'              	JMP	SUBGO
  2903'                         SUBNOP:
  2903'   21 2752'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2906'   C3 2698'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  2909'                         SCOPY:
  2909'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  290B'                         SCP_LP:
  290B'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  290C'   B9                    	CMP	C
  290D'   D2 2926'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  2910'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2911'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2912'   CA 2926'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  2915'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2916'   21 2EA0'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  2919'   BE                    	CMP	M
  291A'   E1                    	POP	H
  291B'   CA 2925'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  291E'   12                    	STAX	D
  291F'   13                    	INX	D
  2920'   23                    	INX	H
  2921'   04                    	INR	B
  2922'   C3 290B'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  2925'                         SCP_SK:
  2925'   23                    	INX	H
  2926'                         SCP_DN:
  2926'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2927'   12                    	STAX	D
  2928'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  2929'                         SUBGO:
  2929'   CD 12DB'              	CALL	GZONE
  292C'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  292F'   22 2EAF'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2932'   21 2EAC'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-128


  2935'   CD 0429'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  2938'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2939'   32 2EA2'              	STA	SANY
  293C'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  293F'   22 2EA4'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2942'   3A 2E9B'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2945'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2946'   CA 2958'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  2949'   CD 2218'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  294C'   DA 29D4'              	JC	SUBBAD
  294F'   2A 2E9C'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  2952'   CD 1249'              	CALL	GOLN
  2955'   C3 295E'              	JMP	SUBLP
  2958'                         SUB_1L:
  2958'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  295B'   22 2EA6'              	SHLD	SLINES
  295E'                         SUBLP:
  295E'   CD 29DA'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2961'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2962'   CA 2970'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  2965'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2967'   32 2EA2'              	STA	SANY
  296A'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  296D'   22 2EA4'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2970'                         SUB_NX:
  2970'   2A 2EA6'              	LHLD	SLINES
  2973'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2974'   22 2EA6'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2977'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2978'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2979'   CA 2997'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  297C'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  297F'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2980'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  2983'   3A 2EA2'              	LDA	SANY
  2986'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2987'   CA 2991'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  298A'   2A 2EA4'              	LHLD	SBACK
  298D'   23                    	INX	H
  298E'   22 2EA4'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  2991'                         SUB_AE:
  2991'   CD 0AE5'              	CALL	ATEOF
  2994'   D2 295E'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  2997'                         SUBDON:
  2997'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  299A'   3A 2EA2'              	LDA	SANY
  299D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  299E'   CA 29BC'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  29A1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29A3'   32 2DDF'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-129


  29A6'   2A 2EA4'              	LHLD	SBACK
  29A9'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  29AA'   B5                    	ORA	L
  29AB'   CA 29B6'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  29AE'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  29B0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  29B3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  29B6'                         SUB_PL:
  29B6'   CD 12F3'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  29B9'   C3 0844'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  29BC'                         SUBNFD:
  29BC'   21 2EAC'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  29BF'   CD 03D0'              	CALL	BACKTO
  29C2'   2A 2EAF'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  29C5'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  29C8'   21 2722'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  29CB'   CD 2698'              	CALL	EXERR
  29CE'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  29D1'   C3 2693'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  29D4'                         SUBBAD:
  29D4'   21 2710'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  29D7'   C3 268D'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  29DA'                         SUBLIN:
  29DA'   AF                    	XRA	A
  29DB'   32 2EA3'              	STA	SLCHG
  29DE'   CD 2A59'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  29E1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  29E4'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  29E4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  29E7'   DA 29EF'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  29EA'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  29EC'   C2 29E4'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  29EF'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  29EF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  29F2'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  29F3'   CD 189D'              	CALL	CEND
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-130


  29F6'   D1                    	POP	D
  29F7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  29FA'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  29FB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  29FE'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  29FF'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2A00'   FA 2A52'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  2A03'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  2A04'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  2A05'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2A08'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  2A0B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2A0E'   DA 2A52'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2A11'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  2A14'   EB                    	XCHG
  2A15'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  2A18'   EB                    	XCHG
  2A19'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2A1C'   EB                    	XCHG
  2A1D'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2A1E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2A21'   D1                    	POP	D
  2A22'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  2A23'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  2A26'   3A 2EA2'              	LDA	SANY
  2A29'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A2A'   C2 2A37'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2A2D'   3A 2EA3'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2A30'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A31'   C2 2A37'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2A34'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  2A37'                         SUBL_C:
  2A37'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  2A3A'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2A3B'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2A3D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2A40'   11 2EB1'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  2A43'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  2A46'                         SUBL_I:
  2A46'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A48'   32 2EA3'              	STA	SLCHG
  2A4B'   3A 2EA1'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2A4E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A4F'   C2 29EF'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2A52'                         SUBL_D:
  2A52'   CD 2A59'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2A55'   3A 2EA3'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2A58'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-131


  2A59'                         SUBBOL:
  2A59'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2A5C'   CD 0624'              	CALL	BOL
  2A5F'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2A62'                         VC_SRCH:
  2A62'   CD 0807'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2A65'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2A66'   CD 0732'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2A69'   22 2E91'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2A6C'   C1                    	POP	B
  2A6D'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2A6E'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2A70'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2A72'   CA 2A77'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2A75'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2A77'                         VS_DIR:
  2A77'   32 2E66'              	STA	SDIR
  2A7A'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2A7D'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2A80'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2A81'   CD 1FC1'              	CALL	RDLINE
  2A84'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A85'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  2A86'   3A 2E3D'              	LDA	RLLEN
  2A89'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A8A'   CA 2A9F'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2A8D'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2A8E'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2A90'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2A93'   19                    	DAD	D
  2A94'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  2A96'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  2A97'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  2A9A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A9C'   32 2E68'              	STA	SHAVE
  2A9F'                         VS_OLD:
  2A9F'   3A 2E68'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2AA2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AA3'   CA 2AAF'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  2AA6'   3A 2E66'              	LDA	SDIR
  2AA9'   32 2E67'              	STA	SDIRE
  2AAC'   C3 2AD8'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2AAF'                         VS_NOP:
  2AAF'   21 2752'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2AB2'   C3 2698'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  2AB5'                         VC_N:
  2AB5'   CD 0807'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2AB8'   3A 2E66'              	LDA	SDIR
  2ABB'   C3 2AC5'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2ABE'                         VCB_N:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-132


  2ABE'   CD 0807'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2AC1'   3A 2E66'              	LDA	SDIR
  2AC4'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  2AC5'                         VN_GO:
  2AC5'   32 2E67'              	STA	SDIRE
  2AC8'   CD 0732'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2ACB'   22 2E91'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2ACE'   3A 2E68'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2AD1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AD2'   CA 2AAF'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  2AD5'   C3 2AD8'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  2AD8'                         SRCHN:
  2AD8'   CD 12DB'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2ADB'   CD 075B'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and an ESC may stop it
  2ADE'                         SN_LP:
  2ADE'   CD 2B13'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2AE1'   DA 2AFE'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2AE4'   2A 2E91'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2AE7'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2AE8'   22 2E91'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2AEB'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2AEC'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2AED'   C2 2ADE'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2AF0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2AF3'   CD 0798'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2AF6'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2AF8'   32 2E73'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2AFB'   C3 12F3'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2AFE'                         SN_BAD:
  2AFE'   CD 0798'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2B01'   DA 07B0'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  2B04'   CD 07A3'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2B07'   21 2722'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2B0A'   CD 2698'              	CALL	EXERR
  2B0D'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2B10'   C3 2693'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2B13'                         SRCHGO:
  2B13'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2B15'   32 2E9A'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  2B18'   CD 2B2E'              	CALL	SRCHW
  2B1B'   D0                    	RNC
  2B1C'   CD 079C'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2B1F'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2B20'   3A 2E67'              	LDA	SDIRE
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-133


  2B23'   2F                    	CMA
  2B24'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2B27'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2B28'   32 2E9A'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  2B2B'   C3 2B2E'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2B2E'                         SRCHW:
  2B2E'   3A 2E67'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2B31'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2B34'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2B37'   22 2DDC'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  2B3A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2B3B'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2B3E'                         SW_LP:
  2B3E'   CD 079C'              	CALL	BRKQ		; an ESC spent the count, here or on the way to
  2B41'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2B42'   CD 2B4E'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2B45'   D0                    	RNC
  2B46'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2B49'   D2 2B3E'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2B4C'   37                    	STC
  2B4D'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2B4E'                         SRCH1:
  2B4E'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2B51'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2B54'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B55'   FA 2B79'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2B58'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2B5B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2B5E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2B61'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2B62'   3A 2E9A'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2B65'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B66'   CA 2B6F'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2B69'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2B6A'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2B6B'   37                    	STC
  2B6C'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2B6D'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2B6E'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-134


  2B6F'                         SR1_FW:
  2B6F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2B72'   D8                    	RC
  2B73'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2B76'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2B79'                         SR1_BK:
  2B79'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2B7C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2B7F'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2B82'   D8                    	RC
  2B83'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2B86'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2B89'   D8                    	RC
  2B8A'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2B8D'                         MSGNAM:
  2B8D'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B8E'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2B90'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2B93'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B94'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2B95'   23                    	INX	H
  2B96'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B97'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B99'   C2 2BA6'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2B9C'   21 27C0'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2B9F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2BA2'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BA3'   C3 2BF0'              	JMP	MN_END
  2BA6'                         MN_HAS:
  2BA6'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2BA8'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2BAB'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BAC'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BAD'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-135


  2BAE'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2BAF'   CA 2BBD'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2BB2'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2BB3'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2BB5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BB8'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2BBA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BBD'                         MN_NAM:
  2BBD'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BBE'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BBF'   23                    	INX	H
  2BC0'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  2BC2'                         MN_NM:
  2BC2'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2BC3'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2BC5'   CA 2BD0'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2BC8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BCB'   23                    	INX	H
  2BCC'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2BCD'   C2 2BC2'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  2BD0'                         MN_EXT:
  2BD0'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BD1'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  2BD4'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  2BD5'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2BD6'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2BD8'   CA 2BF0'              	JZ	MN_END
  2BDB'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2BDD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BE0'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2BE2'                         MN_XL:
  2BE2'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2BE3'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2BE5'   CA 2BF0'              	JZ	MN_END
  2BE8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BEB'   23                    	INX	H
  2BEC'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2BED'   C2 2BE2'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2BF0'                         MN_END:
  2BF0'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2BF2'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2BF5'                         MSGFLG:
  2BF5'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2BF7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BF8'   CA 2C08'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2BFB'   3A 2DDF'              	LDA	MODF
  2BFE'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BFF'   CA 2C08'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2C02'   21 27CA'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2C05'   CD 2C1D'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2C08'                         MF_RO:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-136


  2C08'   3A 2E53'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2C0B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C0C'   CA 2C15'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2C0F'   21 27D5'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2C12'   CD 2C1D'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2C15'                         MF_NEW:
  2C15'   3A 2E51'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2C18'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C19'   C8                    	RZ
  2C1A'   21 27E0'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2C1D'                         MF_PUT:
  2C1D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2C1E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C1F'   C2 2C2B'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2C22'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2C24'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2C25'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2C27'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C2A'   E1                    	POP	H
  2C2B'                         MF_STR:
  2C2B'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2C2E'                         EMITW:
  2C2E'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2C2F'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2C30'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2C32'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2C35'   CD 2C53'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2C38'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2C3B'   CD 2C53'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2C3E'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2C41'   CD 2C53'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2C44'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2C47'   CD 2C53'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2C4A'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2C4B'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2C4D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C50'   D1                    	POP	D
  2C51'   C1                    	POP	B
  2C52'   C9                    	RET
  2C53'                         EWDIG:
  2C53'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2C55'                         EW_LP:
  2C55'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2C56'   93                    	SUB	E
  2C57'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2C58'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2C59'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2C5A'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2C5B'   DA 2C62'              	JC	EW_DN
  2C5E'   0C                    	INR	C
  2C5F'   C3 2C55'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2C62'                         EW_DN:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-137


  2C62'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2C63'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2C64'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C65'   C2 2C6B'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2C68'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2C69'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C6A'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2C6B'                         EW_PR:
  2C6B'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2C6C'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2C6E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C71'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2C73'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2C74'                         ISEMPT:
  2C74'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2C77'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2C78'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2C79'   C0                    	RNZ
  2C7A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2C7D'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2C7E'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2C7F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2C80'                         MSGCTG:
  2C80'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2C83'   CD 2B8D'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2C86'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2C88'   CD 2BF5'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2C8B'   CD 2C74'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2C8E'   C2 2C97'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2C91'   21 27F3'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2C94'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2C97'                         MG_POS:
  2C97'   21 27E6'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2C9A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2C9D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2CA0'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2CA1'   CD 2C2E'              	CALL	EMITW
  2CA4'   21 27ED'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2CA7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2CAA'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2CAD'   23                    	INX	H
  2CAE'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2CAF'   CD 2C2E'              	CALL	EMITW
  2CB2'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2CB5'   23                    	INX	H
  2CB6'   D1                    	POP	D
  2CB7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2CBA'   C8                    	RZ
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-138


  2CBB'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2CBC'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2CBE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2CC1'   E1                    	POP	H
  2CC2'   C3 2C2E'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2CC5'                         FILMSG:
  2CC5'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2CC8'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2CCA'   C8                    	RZ
  2CCB'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2CCE'   CD 2B8D'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2CD1'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  2CD2'   C3 2BF5'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  2CD5'                         WRMSG:
  2CD5'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2CD8'   CD 2B8D'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2CDB'   21 2805'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2CDE'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2CE1'                         MSGPST:
  2CE1'   3A 2E50'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2CE4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2CE5'   C8                    	RZ
  2CE6'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2CE7'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2CE8'   32 2E50'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2CEB'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2CEC'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2CEE'   CA 2D0A'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2CF1'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2CF3'   CA 2D10'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2CF6'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  2CF8'   CA 2D01'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2CFB'   CD 2C80'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2CFE'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2D01'                         MP_BIG:
  2D01'   21 27A1'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  2D04'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2D07'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2D0A'                         MP_FIL:
  2D0A'   CD 2CC5'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2D0D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2D10'                         MP_WRT:
  2D10'   CD 2CD5'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2D13'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-139


                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2D16'                         VC_CTLG:
  2D16'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2D18'   32 2E50'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2D1B'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2D1D'   32 2DE0'              	STA	RINTENT
  2D20'   C3 0732'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  2D23'                         CMDTAB:
  2D23'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  2D24'   2D16'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
